---
import { getCollection } from 'astro:content';
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

// The atlas in numbers, computed from the content at every build so the
// figures can never go stale.
const [civs, langs, names] = await Promise.all([
  getCollection('civilizations'),
  getCollection('languages'),
  getCollection('names'),
]);
const citations = names.reduce((n, e) => n + e.data.sources.length, 0);
const complete = civs.filter((c) => c.data.entry_status === 'complete').length;
---

<BaseLayout title="About" description="Methodology and editorial conventions of Onomastikon.">
  <article class="prose">
    <p class="eyebrow">About</p>
    <h1>Methodology</h1>

    <p class="lede">
      Onomastikon is a digital reference work created by Shriram Rajagopal that catalogs the endonyms and exonyms
      of ancient entities, including civilizations, cities, regions, and geographic features. This includes what they called 
      themselves and what their neighbors called them. Each entry records the original script,
      transliteration, reconstructed pronunciation, literal meaning, and primary
      sources, with attention to uncertainty.
    </p>

    <ul class="stats" aria-label="The atlas in numbers">
      <li><strong>{civs.length}</strong> entities<br /><span>{complete} complete</span></li>
      <li><strong>{langs.length}</strong> languages<br /><span>ancient scripts rendered</span></li>
      <li><strong>{names.length}</strong> name entries<br /><span>two-paragraph treatments</span></li>
      <li><strong>{citations}</strong> citations<br /><span>sources on every entry</span></li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Editorial scope</h2>
    <p>
      The project covers entities of the ancient world, including civilizations, cities, regions, 
      and geographic features. The temporal scope runs from the Bronze Age to the fall of Constantinople in 1453.
      Each entry focuses on its subject as a primarily linguistic but also historical record.
      Readers seeking detailed historical context should consult the standard reference works cited in each entry.
    </p>
    <h3>One page per name-thread</h3>
    <p>
      Entities are split by name-tradition, not by polity. Egypt spans thirty
      dynasties on one page because <em>Kemet</em> is one continuous thread;
      Persia carries <em>Pārsa</em> from the Achaemenids through the Sasanians,
      while the Sasanian state-name <em>Ērānšahr</em> belongs to the separate
      Iran entry. A polity earns its own page only where it introduced a
      genuinely new endonym thread, never one page per dynasty.
    </p>
    <h3>Regions and identification across languages</h3>
    <p>
      Regional names attested in different languages do not always pick out
      identical geographic units. Greek <em>Mesopotamía</em>, Akkadian
      <em>Māt birīt nārim</em>, and Hebrew <em>Aram-Naharaim</em> all refer
      transparently to the land between the Tigris and the Euphrates, and are
      treated as variant names of the same entity. By contrast, a single root
      can fan out into several distinct entities when it comes to name different
      <em>kinds</em> of unit. Sanskrit <em>Sindhu</em>, the great river, gave its
      name through Old Persian <em>Hinduš</em> to a whole subcontinent; the river
      itself (the Indus), the lower-river region (Sindh), and the civilization
      (India, whose own endonym is <em>Bhārata</em>) are recorded as three
      separate entities, since a river, a region, and a civilization are not
      interchangeable even where their names share a root. Where two names
      overlap partially or are framed differently, the entry's prose explains
      the distinction.
    </p>
    <h3>Entry status</h3>
    <p>
      Each entity entry carries a status label. <span class="status status-developing">Developing</span> indicates that an
      entry is still being expanded with additional languages and sources.
      <span class="status status-complete">Complete</span> indicates an entry with the full range of languages
      with attested names for that entity.
    </p>

    <h2>Name families and transmission</h2>
    <p>
      Each entity page groups its names into families by shared root and draws a
      transmission map of how those names traveled, and the two views encode
      different relationships. In a family tree, an indented form is a
      <em>borrowing</em>, one name taken from the one above it (Latin
      <em>Aegyptus</em> from Greek <em>Aígyptos</em>); forms at the same level
      are <em>cognates</em>, parallel reflexes of a common ancestor that no
      language took from any other (the Semitic names for Egypt, each inheriting
      the root <em>mṣr</em> in its own line). The map draws only borrowings as
      arcs: cognate forms share a family and a homeland cluster but are not
      joined by a line, because no name passed between them. A page can therefore
      show a whole family with no connecting arcs at all, where every form is a
      parallel inheritance rather than a borrowing. A borrowing is recorded only
      where an entry's own prose asserts it without hedging.
    </p>

    <h2>Source tiers</h2>
    <p>
      Entries draw on sources in roughly the following order of priority, with
      higher tiers preferred wherever they offer adequate coverage.
    </p>
    <ol>
      <li>
        <strong>Standard reference works in the relevant philological tradition:</strong>
        the <em>Oxford Classical Dictionary</em> (4th ed.), the
        <em>Cambridge Ancient History</em>, the <em>Reallexikon der
        Assyriologie</em>, the <em>Lexikon der Ägyptologie</em>, and the
        <em>Encyclopaedia Iranica</em>.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Specialist lexica and grammars:</strong> the Chicago Assyrian
        Dictionary (CAD), Liddell–Scott–Jones, Beekes' <em>Etymological
        Dictionary of Greek</em>, the <em>Hebrew and Aramaic Lexicon of the Old
        Testament</em> (HALOT), and comparable works for each language tradition.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Primary text corpora:</strong> the Cuneiform Digital Library
        Initiative (CDLI), the <em>Thesaurus Linguae Aegyptiae</em>, the
        Perseus Digital Library, the <em>Thesaurus Linguae Graecae</em>,
        and ORACC.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Peer-reviewed monographs and journal articles</strong> for
        contested cases or recent reassessments.
      </li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      Wikipedia and other tertiary aggregators are used as starting points for
      navigation but never as citations.
    </p>

    <h2>Reconstruction and uncertainty</h2>
    <p>
      Reconstructed pronunciations follow the standard linguistic convention of
      a leading asterisk (e.g., <em>*kuːmat</em>). Where multiple reconstructions
      are current, the entry follows the source cited and notes the alternative(s).
      All dates are approximate, as denoted by "c." (circa). Date ranges represent
      attested periods of time in which names were used. For instance, Latin existed
      for roughly a millennium before <em>Aegyptus</em> appears in Latin sources as a name 
      for Egypt. So the entry dates reflect the narrower window of 
      the name's use rather than the full lifespan of the language to which it belongs.
      Where a name's first attestation is securely dated to a specific inscription or
      text, the entry notes this and specifies the date of the source.
    </p>

     <p>
      Every name entry carries one of three confidence labels:
    </p>
    <dl>
      <dt><span class="confidence confidence-attested">Attested</span></dt>
      <dd>The form is documented in primary sources, even where its
      pronunciation must be reconstructed (as is typical for Egyptian and
      cuneiform corpora).</dd>
      <dt><span class="confidence confidence-reconstructed">Reconstructed</span></dt>
      <dd>The form itself, not merely its pronunciation, is inferred, typically
      through comparative reconstruction or projection from a later attested
      form.</dd>
      <dt><span class="confidence confidence-disputed">Disputed</span></dt>
      <dd>Scholars disagree substantively about the form, its meaning, or its
      identification with the entity in question.</dd>
    </dl>

    <h2>Transliteration conventions</h2>
    <p>
      Each language follows the transliteration standard of its established
      philological tradition: the Leiden Unified Transliteration for Ancient
      Egyptian; the Chicago system for Akkadian (CAD); the Library of Congress
      ALA-LC romanization for Sanskrit; standard scholarly conventions for
      Greek, Latin, Old Persian, and Biblical Hebrew. Where multiple systems
      coexist, the entry notes the choice.
    </p>

    <h2>Limitations</h2>
    <p>
      Onomastikon is the work of a student in active study, not a credentialed
      philologist. Entries are checked against the cited sources and corrected
      when error is identified. If you spot an error or have a suggestion, the
      <a href="/feedback">feedback page</a> is the place to say so; each entry's
      source Markdown can also be viewed, cited, and improved by pull request on
      the project's GitHub repository.
    </p>

    <h2>Source and data</h2>
    <p>
      The full source of the project, including the underlying data for every
      entry, is available at
      <a href="https://github.com/shriram-rajagopal/onomastikon">github.com/shriram-rajagopal/onomastikon</a>.
      The complete dataset (every entity, language, and name record with its
      sources) is also published machine-readable at
      <a href="/onomastikon.json">onomastikon.org/onomastikon.json</a>.
      Code is released under the MIT license; content and data under
      <a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/">Creative Commons
      Attribution-ShareAlike 4.0</a>.
    </p>
    <p>
      Each entity and language page carries a "Cite this page" block with a
      formatted citation and a BibTeX record.
    </p>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .prose { max-width: 36rem; }
  .eyebrow {
    font-family: 'Inter', sans-serif;
    font-size: 0.75rem;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.1em;
    color: var(--ink-soft);
    margin: 0 0 0.5rem;
  }
  .lede {
    font-size: 1.25rem;
    line-height: 1.5;
    color: var(--ink-soft);
    margin: 1.5rem 0 2.5rem;
  }
  .prose p { margin: 1rem 0; }
  .stats {
    display: grid;
    /* Two by two: the four figures sit as a balanced square. */
    grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
    gap: 1.4rem 0.9rem;
    list-style: none;
    max-width: 24rem;
    margin-inline: auto;
    padding: 1.5rem 1.1rem;
    margin-bottom: 2.5rem;
    border: 1px solid var(--rule-strong);
    border-radius: 6px;
    background-image: var(--grain);
    box-shadow: var(--shadow-soft);
    font-family: 'Inter', sans-serif;
    font-size: 0.75rem;
    color: var(--ink-soft);
    text-align: center;
  }
  .stats strong {
    display: block;
    font-family: 'EB Garamond', Georgia, serif;
    font-size: 1.9rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    color: var(--accent);
    line-height: 1.1;
  }
  .prose h3 {
    font-family: 'Inter', sans-serif;
    font-size: 0.875rem;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.08em;
    color: var(--ink-soft);
    margin: 2rem 0 0.75rem;
  }
  .prose ol, .prose ul { padding-left: 1.5rem; }
  .prose li { margin: 0.5rem 0; }
  .prose dl {
    margin: 1.5rem 0;
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: max-content 1fr;
    gap: 0.5rem 1.5rem;
    align-items: start;
  }
  .prose dt { padding-top: 0.15rem; }
  .prose dd { margin: 0; }
  .confidence {
    font-family: 'Inter', sans-serif;
    font-size: 0.75rem;
    padding: 0.15rem 0.5rem;
    border-radius: 3px;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.05em;
  }
  .confidence-attested { background: var(--conf-attested-bg); color: var(--conf-attested-fg); }
  .confidence-reconstructed { background: var(--conf-reconstructed-bg); color: var(--conf-reconstructed-fg); }
  .confidence-disputed { background: var(--conf-disputed-bg); color: var(--conf-disputed-fg); }
  .status {
    font-family: 'Inter', sans-serif;
    font-size: 0.6875rem;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.06em;
    padding: 0.1rem 0.4rem;
    border-radius: 3px;
    vertical-align: middle;
  }
  .status-developing { background: var(--badge-developing-bg); color: var(--badge-developing-fg); }
  .status-complete { background: var(--badge-complete-bg); color: var(--badge-complete-fg); }
</style>